{
  "model": "medium",
  "language": "en",
  "text": "3.5. There is no doubt that antibiotics are one of the most important developments in the entire history of medicine. Before antibiotics, what we would now think of as minor problems, like having a small paper cut which went on to get infected, had the possibility to kill. But everything changed in 1928, when a researcher named Dr. Alexander Fleming, who was investigating how bacteria developed and grew, came back from vacation. He found mold growing in a dish he'd left behind in his laboratory, a mold he later named penicillin. The dish had bacteria on it, and the mold was stopping the bacteria from growing as it normally would. The penicillin was gradually developed into antibiotic drugs, which allowed people to recover quickly and easily from infection. Antibiotics have saved countless lives. However, using them has also created a massive problem. Bacteria constantly change and develop. They find ways to survive. Indeed, they become resistant to the medicines we use to fight them, to the extent that our medicines just aren't effective anymore. Quite simply, the more antibiotics we use, the stronger bacteria become, leading to super bacteria that might never have existed if we hadn't started using antibiotics. It's estimated that resistant bacteria infect 800,000 people a year, killing 100 every day in Europe alone. If we don't do something about the problem, there could be a huge outbreak of illness each year because of bacteria that we no longer have the power to fight.",
